<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eanna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as E-anna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eanna, 'House of Heaven', was the great temple precinct of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> in the Sumerian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>. Its monumental buildings of the 4th millennium BCE, decorated with cone mosaics, are among the earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and they yielded the oldest proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warka-vase">Warka Vase</a>. Kings rebuilt it for some three thousand years, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a> praises Uruk's walls and Eanna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eanna.jpg" alt="Eanna" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Picture taken by Marcus Cyron · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Part_of_front_of_Inanna_temple_of_Kara_Indasch_from_Uruk_Vorderasiatisches_Museum_Berlin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
